import BlogLayout from '../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro';

const bodyContent = `<p>You have three agents. Agent A monitors CPU. Agent B monitors memory. Agent C needs to aggregate both streams and trigger alerts. The conventional answer is <a href="https://kafka.apache.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kafka</a>, <a href="https://www.rabbitmq.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RabbitMQ</a>, or <a href="https://redis.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Redis</a> Streams. That means another service to deploy, configure, secure, and keep running. For a handful of agents exchanging status events, that is a lot of infrastructure.</p>

  <p>Every Pilot daemon already runs a small pub/sub broker on <strong>port 1002</strong> (the <a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol/eventstream" target="_blank" rel="noopener">eventstream</a> plugin, on unless the daemon is started with <code>-no-eventstream</code>). Pick one daemon as the meeting point, and the other agents subscribe and publish to it over Pilot's encrypted tunnels. From the shell that is <code>pilotctl subscribe &lt;peer&gt; &lt;topic&gt;</code> and <code>pilotctl publish &lt;peer&gt; &lt;topic&gt; --data …</code>. From Go it is the <code>eventstream</code> package, and a working subscriber fits in about a dozen lines.</p>

  <section>
  <h2>The Problem with Brokers for Agents</h2>

  <p>Message brokers are built for microservices running in the same data center. They assume:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>All producers and consumers can reach the broker over TCP</li>
    <li>The broker runs on infrastructure you control</li>
    <li>You can manage topics, partitions, and consumer groups</li>
    <li>You need persistence, replay, and exactly-once delivery</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Agent networks break most of these assumptions. Agents sit behind NAT. They run on laptops, VMs, and edge devices. They join and leave dynamically. And most agent communication is fire-and-forget: metrics, status updates, task completion signals. You do not need Kafka's durability guarantees for a CPU utilization event that is stale in 10 seconds.</p>

  <p>What you need is <strong>topic routing between agents that can reach each other</strong>, over connections that work through NAT, with encryption that does not require certificate management. Port 1002 gives you that, with one condition: one of the daemons acts as the broker.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>How the Built-In Broker Works</h2>

  <p>There is still a broker. It is just not a separate service: it is part of the Pilot daemon you already run. The rules are short, and all of them come from the broker's source (<code>service.go</code> in the eventstream repository):</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>The first event on a connection is its subscription.</strong> Its topic is either an exact topic name or <code>*</code> for every topic. Every later event on that connection is a publication.</li>
    <li><strong>Topics match exactly.</strong> A subscriber to <code>metrics.cpu</code> gets <code>metrics.cpu</code> and nothing else. There are no prefix wildcards: <code>metrics.*</code> is just a topic called <code>metrics.*</code>. Only <code>*</code> on its own means "everything".</li>
    <li><strong>Fan-out, not queues.</strong> A publication goes to every subscriber of its topic plus every <code>*</code> subscriber, except the connection that sent it.</li>
    <li><strong>Fire-and-forget.</strong> Nothing is stored. A subscriber that is not connected when an event is published never sees it.</li>
    <li><strong>Limits.</strong> Each publishing connection may send 100 events per second, with bursts of up to 200; events over the limit are dropped. A topic can have up to 1,000 subscribers. Topic names are capped at 1,024 bytes and payloads at 16 MiB.</li>
    <li><strong>Slow subscribers.</strong> A failed write is retried once. After three failed or timed-out (5 s) writes in a row, the broker closes that subscriber's connection.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Who can connect depends on the hub daemon's visibility. Port 1002 is reached over a Pilot tunnel, and a private daemon (the default) only accepts tunnels from peers it trusts or shares a network with. So a private hub serves only agents that have completed a <a href="trust-model-agents-invisible-by-default">trust handshake</a> with it. A public daemon accepts tunnels from anyone, and the broker's default topic policy allows any topic, so keep a hub private unless you want an open channel.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>The Twelve-Line Subscriber</h2>

  <p>Here is a complete subscriber. The body of <code>main</code> is twelve lines plus a closing brace, with the error checks written on one line each (<code>gofmt</code> would spread them out).</p>

  <pre><code>package main

import (
    "fmt"
    "log"
    "os"

    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/driver"
    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/protocol"
    "github.com/pilot-protocol/eventstream"
)

func main() {
    d, err := driver.Connect("")                       <span class="comment">// local daemon's IPC socket</span>
    if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }
    defer d.Close()
    hub, err := protocol.ParseAddr(os.Args[1])         <span class="comment">// e.g. 0:0000.0000.1A2B</span>
    if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }
    sub, err := eventstream.Subscribe(d, hub, "metrics.cpu")
    if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }
    defer sub.Close()
    for {
        evt, err := sub.Recv()
        if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }
        fmt.Printf("[%s] %s\\n", evt.Topic, evt.Payload)
    }
}</code></pre>

  <p><code>driver.Connect("")</code> connects to the local Pilot daemon over its IPC socket (the default path when the argument is empty). <code>eventstream.Subscribe</code> opens a stream to port 1002 on the hub and sends the subscription. <code>Recv</code> blocks until the next event and returns its <code>Topic</code> and <code>Payload</code>.</p>

  <p>The publisher is the same connection used the other way. A connection must subscribe before it publishes, so a publish-only process subscribes to a topic of its own (here a control topic for that agent) and then calls <code>Publish</code> with any topic it likes:</p>

  <pre><code>    c, err := eventstream.Subscribe(d, hub, "control.agent-a")
    if err != nil { log.Fatal(err) }
    defer c.Close()
    for {
        c.Publish("metrics.cpu", []byte(\`{"percent":42.0}\`))
        time.Sleep(5 * time.Second)
    }</code></pre>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>Why not subscribe the publisher to <code>metrics.cpu</code>?</strong> It would then receive every other agent's <code>metrics.cpu</code> events on that connection. If the process never reads them, they pile up, and the broker drops a connection whose writes keep failing. Subscribe a publish-only connection to a topic nobody else publishes on, or read and discard what arrives.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <p><strong>What comes with the tunnel:</strong> encryption (X25519 key agreement, AES-256-GCM), NAT traversal (STUN, hole punching, relay fallback), address resolution through the registry, and trust: a private hub only talks to peers it has approved. No TLS certificates and no firewall rules.</p>
  </div>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>Build a Monitoring Pipeline</h2>

  <p>Let us build something concrete. Three agents and a hub:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hub</strong>: any Pilot daemon all three agents trust, for example a small VM that only runs <code>pilotctl daemon start</code>. Its address is passed to each agent as the first argument.</li>
    <li><strong>Agent A</strong> (us-east): publishes CPU metrics every 5 seconds to <code>metrics.cpu</code></li>
    <li><strong>Agent B</strong> (eu-west): publishes memory metrics every 5 seconds to <code>metrics.memory</code></li>
    <li><strong>Agent C</strong> (us-central): subscribes to both topics, aggregates, and publishes alerts to <code>alerts.cpu</code></li>
  </ul>

  <p>Each agent runs on its own daemon and has completed a handshake with the hub (<code>pilotctl handshake &lt;hub-address&gt;</code> on the agent, <code>pilotctl approve &lt;node_id&gt;</code> on the hub).</p>

  <h3>Agent A: CPU Publisher</h3>

  <pre><code>package main

import (
    "encoding/json"
    "log"
    "os"
    "os/exec"
    "strconv"
    "strings"
    "time"

    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/driver"
    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/protocol"
    "github.com/pilot-protocol/eventstream"
)

type CPUMetric struct {
    Host    string  \`json:"host"\`
    Percent float64 \`json:"percent"\`
    Ts      int64   \`json:"ts"\`
}

func main() {
    d, err := driver.Connect("")
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    defer d.Close()

    hub, err := protocol.ParseAddr(os.Args[1])
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    host, _ := os.Hostname()

    <span class="comment">// Subscribe to this agent's own control topic, then publish metrics.</span>
    c, err := eventstream.Subscribe(d, hub, "control."+host)
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    defer c.Close()

    for {
        data, _ := json.Marshal(CPUMetric{
            Host:    host,
            Percent: readCPU(),
            Ts:      time.Now().Unix(),
        })
        if err := c.Publish("metrics.cpu", data); err != nil {
            log.Fatal(err) <span class="comment">// connection closed; restart and resubscribe</span>
        }
        time.Sleep(5 * time.Second)
    }
}

func readCPU() float64 {
    <span class="comment">// Linux: user CPU percentage from top</span>
    out, _ := exec.Command("sh", "-c",
        "top -bn1 | grep 'Cpu(s)' | awk '{print $2}'").Output()
    val, _ := strconv.ParseFloat(strings.TrimSpace(string(out)), 64)
    return val
}</code></pre>

  <h3>Agent B: Memory Publisher</h3>

  <pre><code>package main

import (
    "encoding/json"
    "log"
    "os"
    "runtime"
    "time"

    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/driver"
    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/protocol"
    "github.com/pilot-protocol/eventstream"
)

type MemMetric struct {
    Host    string \`json:"host"\`
    AllocMB uint64 \`json:"alloc_mb"\`
    TotalMB uint64 \`json:"total_mb"\`
    Ts      int64  \`json:"ts"\`
}

func main() {
    d, err := driver.Connect("")
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    defer d.Close()

    hub, err := protocol.ParseAddr(os.Args[1])
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    host, _ := os.Hostname()

    c, err := eventstream.Subscribe(d, hub, "control."+host)
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    defer c.Close()

    for {
        var m runtime.MemStats
        runtime.ReadMemStats(&m)

        data, _ := json.Marshal(MemMetric{
            Host:    host,
            AllocMB: m.Alloc / 1024 / 1024,
            TotalMB: m.TotalAlloc / 1024 / 1024,
            Ts:      time.Now().Unix(),
        })
        if err := c.Publish("metrics.memory", data); err != nil {
            log.Fatal(err)
        }
        time.Sleep(5 * time.Second)
    }
}</code></pre>

  <h3>Agent C: Aggregator and Alerter</h3>

  <p>Topics match exactly, so Agent C opens one subscription per topic and merges them into one channel.</p>

  <pre><code>package main

import (
    "encoding/json"
    "fmt"
    "log"
    "os"
    "time"

    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/driver"
    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/protocol"
    "github.com/pilot-protocol/eventstream"
)

type Alert struct {
    Level   string \`json:"level"\`
    Message string \`json:"message"\`
    Source  string \`json:"source"\`
    Ts      int64  \`json:"ts"\`
}

func main() {
    d, err := driver.Connect("")
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    defer d.Close()

    hub, err := protocol.ParseAddr(os.Args[1])
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }

    <span class="comment">// One connection per exact topic, merged into one channel.</span>
    events := make(chan *eventstream.Event)
    var cpu *eventstream.Client
    for _, topic := range []string{"metrics.cpu", "metrics.memory"} {
        c, err := eventstream.Subscribe(d, hub, topic)
        if err != nil {
            log.Fatal(err)
        }
        defer c.Close()
        if topic == "metrics.cpu" {
            cpu = c
        }
        go func(c *eventstream.Client) {
            for {
                evt, err := c.Recv()
                if err != nil {
                    log.Fatal(err)
                }
                events <- evt
            }
        }(c)
    }

    fmt.Println("Aggregator listening on metrics.cpu and metrics.memory")

    for evt := range events {
        switch evt.Topic {
        case "metrics.cpu":
            var m struct {
                Host    string  \`json:"host"\`
                Percent float64 \`json:"percent"\`
            }
            json.Unmarshal(evt.Payload, &m)
            fmt.Printf("[CPU] %s: %.1f%%\\n", m.Host, m.Percent)

            if m.Percent > 90.0 {
                alert := Alert{
                    Level:   "critical",
                    Message: fmt.Sprintf("CPU at %.1f%% on %s", m.Percent, m.Host),
                    Source:  m.Host,
                    Ts:      time.Now().Unix(),
                }
                data, _ := json.Marshal(alert)
                <span class="comment">// Any connection can publish to any topic.</span>
                cpu.Publish("alerts.cpu", data)
                fmt.Printf("[ALERT] %s\\n", alert.Message)
            }

        case "metrics.memory":
            var m struct {
                Host    string \`json:"host"\`
                AllocMB uint64 \`json:"alloc_mb"\`
            }
            json.Unmarshal(evt.Payload, &m)
            fmt.Printf("[MEM] %s: %d MB\\n", m.Host, m.AllocMB)
        }
    }
}</code></pre>

  <p>When CPU exceeds 90%, Agent C publishes an alert to <code>alerts.cpu</code>. Another agent can subscribe to <code>alerts.cpu</code> on the same hub to trigger remediation: scale down a workload, notify a human, or restart the offending process. If the hub carries nothing else, Agent C could instead subscribe once to <code>*</code> and switch on <code>evt.Topic</code>; with <code>*</code> it also receives every other topic on that hub.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>Build a Workflow Pipeline</h2>

  <p>Event streams are not just for metrics. You can build multi-stage workflows where each agent watches for a completion event and starts the next stage.</p>

  <h3>The Pipeline</h3>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Agent 1 (Ingestion):</strong> Receives raw data, normalizes it, publishes <code>pipeline.stage1.complete</code></li>
    <li><strong>Agent 2 (Analysis):</strong> Subscribes to <code>pipeline.stage1.complete</code>, runs analysis, publishes <code>pipeline.stage2.complete</code></li>
    <li><strong>Agent 3 (Summary):</strong> Subscribes to <code>pipeline.stage2.complete</code>, generates a summary with an LLM, publishes <code>pipeline.stage3.complete</code></li>
    <li><strong>Agent 4 (Output):</strong> Subscribes to <code>pipeline.stage3.complete</code>, writes the final report</li>
  </ol>

  <p>Here is Agent 2 as an example. The others follow the same pattern.</p>

  <pre><code>package main

import (
    "encoding/json"
    "fmt"
    "log"
    "os"

    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/driver"
    "github.com/pilot-protocol/common/protocol"
    "github.com/pilot-protocol/eventstream"
)

type StageEvent struct {
    Stage   int             \`json:"stage"\`
    JobID   string          \`json:"job_id"\`
    Payload json.RawMessage \`json:"payload"\`
}

func main() {
    d, err := driver.Connect("")
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    defer d.Close()
    hub, err := protocol.ParseAddr(os.Args[1])
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }

    <span class="comment">// Wait for stage 1 to complete</span>
    c, err := eventstream.Subscribe(d, hub, "pipeline.stage1.complete")
    if err != nil {
        log.Fatal(err)
    }
    defer c.Close()

    fmt.Println("Agent 2 (Analysis) waiting for stage 1...")

    for {
        evt, err := c.Recv()
        if err != nil {
            log.Fatal(err)
        }
        var prev StageEvent
        json.Unmarshal(evt.Payload, &prev)

        fmt.Printf("Received job %s from stage 1\\n", prev.JobID)

        <span class="comment">// Do analysis work</span>
        result := analyze(prev.Payload)

        <span class="comment">// Signal the next stage on the same connection</span>
        data, _ := json.Marshal(StageEvent{
            Stage:   2,
            JobID:   prev.JobID,
            Payload: result,
        })
        c.Publish("pipeline.stage2.complete", data)

        fmt.Printf("Job %s: stage 2 complete\\n", prev.JobID)
    }
}

func analyze(raw json.RawMessage) json.RawMessage {
    <span class="comment">// Your analysis logic here</span>
    result := map[string]any{
        "analyzed": true,
        "findings": []string{"pattern_a detected", "anomaly in sector 7"},
    }
    data, _ := json.Marshal(result)
    return data
}</code></pre>

  <p>Each agent is independent. Agent 2 does not know about Agent 3. It just publishes a completion event. If Agent 3 is down, the event is lost: this is fire-and-forget pub/sub, not a durable queue. When Agent 3 comes back and subscribes again, it picks up new events. If a lost job matters, have the stage that owns it re-publish until the next stage confirms, or keep jobs in a store and use events only as wake-up signals.</p>

  <h3>Branching Pipelines</h3>

  <p>Suppose stage 2 can produce two kinds of output: normal results and anomalies. Publish them to different topics:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment">// Normal result</span>
c.Publish("pipeline.stage2.result.normal", data)

<span class="comment">// Anomaly detected</span>
c.Publish("pipeline.stage2.result.anomaly", data)</code></pre>

  <p>An agent that handles only anomalies subscribes to <code>pipeline.stage2.result.anomaly</code>. An agent that handles both opens one subscription per topic, as Agent C does above. Because there are no prefix wildcards, the routing table is the explicit list of topics each agent subscribes to, which also makes it easy to read.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>CLI Equivalent for Quick Testing</h2>

  <p>You do not need to write Go to test the event stream. <code>pilotctl</code> has publish and subscribe commands that speak the same protocol.</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Terminal 1: subscribe to CPU metrics on the hub</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl subscribe &lt;hub-address&gt; metrics.cpu</span>

<span class="comment"># Terminal 2: publish a CPU metric</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl publish &lt;hub-address&gt; metrics.cpu --data '{"host":"agent-a","percent":73.2}'</span>

<span class="comment"># Terminal 3: publish a memory metric (terminal 1 does not see this one)</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl publish &lt;hub-address&gt; metrics.memory --data '{"host":"agent-b","alloc_mb":256}'</span></code></pre>

  <p>Terminal 1 prints each matching event as <code>[topic] payload</code>:</p>

  <pre><code>[metrics.cpu] {"host":"agent-a","percent":73.2}</code></pre>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Subscribe to everything on the hub (useful for debugging)</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl subscribe &lt;hub-address&gt; '*'</span>

<span class="comment"># Stop after 10 events, or after 60 s</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl subscribe &lt;hub-address&gt; pipeline.stage2.result.anomaly --count 10 --timeout 60s</span>

<span class="comment"># Machine-readable: one JSON object per event</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl --json subscribe &lt;hub-address&gt; metrics.cpu</span></code></pre>

  <p>The hub can be given as an address or, once you trust it, as its hostname. Use the CLI to try out your topic names before writing Go code.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>When You Still Need Kafka</h2>

  <p>Pilot's event stream is <strong>not</strong> a replacement for Kafka in all scenarios. You still need a real broker when:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Persistence:</strong> Events must survive restarts. Pilot's stream keeps nothing. If the subscriber is offline when an event is published, that event is gone.</li>
    <li><strong>Replay:</strong> You need to re-process events from a specific offset. There is no event log.</li>
    <li><strong>Consumer groups:</strong> Multiple instances of the same subscriber need to split the workload. Pilot delivers every event to every subscriber of its topic, not to one of many.</li>
    <li><strong>Delivery guarantees:</strong> The broker gives at-most-once delivery. Events published during a disconnect, over the rate limit, or to a subscriber whose writes keep failing are dropped.</li>
    <li><strong>Volume:</strong> Each publishing connection is limited to 100 events per second (bursts of 200). Workloads far beyond that belong on infrastructure built for them.</li>
    <li><strong>Pattern subscriptions:</strong> If you depend on topic wildcards or hierarchies, use a broker that has them.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>But here is the question to ask yourself: does your agent system actually need any of those? Most agent event traffic is:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Status updates (fire-and-forget)</li>
    <li>Task completion signals (at-most-once is fine when the application handles retries)</li>
    <li>Metrics (stale data is replaced by fresh data)</li>
    <li>Coordination signals ("I am ready", "start next phase")</li>
  </ul>

  <p>For these patterns, the broker already in your Pilot daemon is often enough. Save Kafka for when you actually need Kafka.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
  <h2>Putting It Together</h2>

  <p>Start with the CLI to try out your topic names. Move to Go when you need programmatic control. Pick a hub daemon every agent can trust, open one subscription per exact topic, and publish completion events to chain stages. When a topic outgrows the rate limit or needs durability, move that topic to a dedicated broker and keep Pilot for the coordination traffic.</p>

  <p>The event stream is one of the daemon's built-in services. Port 1001 carries <code>send-message</code> and <code>send-file</code> (data exchange), port 1002 carries pub/sub, and the <a href="http-services-over-encrypted-overlay">gateway</a> can carry ordinary HTTP over the overlay.</p>

  <p>For the reference, see the <a href="/docs/pubsub">pub/sub documentation</a>. The Go sources are the <code>eventstream</code> package (<a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol/eventstream" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/pilot-protocol/eventstream</a>) and the driver in <code>common/driver</code> (<a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol/common" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/pilot-protocol/common</a>).</p>
  </section>

  <div class="cta">
    <h3>Try the Event Stream</h3>
    <p>Three terminals and two commands: subscribe in one, publish from another, and watch the event arrive.</p>
    <a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol/eventstream">View on GitHub</a>
  </div>`;
